# Textarea

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A textarea is a labelled box where a person types an answer that needs more than one line, such as a dashboard description or a short household instruction. Use it for a specific answer, with a hint that says what to write and who can read it.

Do not use an open box when a small set of choices would answer the question more reliably. Do not suggest that a note is read by a support team. For a short answer on one line, use [Text input](text-input.html).

This page covers:

- `<textarea>`
- `ha-textarea`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

From top to bottom:

1. A visible [label](label.html) that names the answer.
2. A short [hint](hint-text.html) that says what to write and who will see it.
3. The box, which holds several lines and grows or scrolls as the text gets longer.
4. An error message, shown only when something is wrong, linked to the field.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-textarea"><code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code></h3>

The native multi-line field. It takes plain text, and its starting text is placed inside the tag. Useful attributes are `rows` (two by default), `maxlength`, `minlength`, `placeholder`, `required`, `readonly`, `disabled`, `autocomplete`, `spellcheck` and `wrap`.

- Set a starting height that fits the expected answer, about four rows for a description, and let people resize it vertically.
- Name it with a `<label for>` and link the hint and any error with `aria-describedby`. Mark it with `aria-invalid` when the answer has a problem.
- Tab moves focus out of the box. It does not insert a tab, so keyboard users are not trapped.
- Keep paste and the usual editing shortcuts working.
- A fixed-height box must scroll or grow when text is larger or spaced out.
- Do not use a placeholder in place of a label.

A dashboard author cannot write a `<textarea>`: a Markdown card shows the tag as literal text instead of a box.

<h3 id="child-ha-textarea"><code>ha-textarea</code></h3>

The multi-line field Home Assistant draws. It is not a dashboard card, so it has no `type:` key. It appears in dialogs, such as saving an automation or editing a label, and the `text` selector draws it when `multiline: true` is set.

```yaml
selector:
  text:
    multiline: true
```

In the selector the box grows with the text and spellcheck and automatic capital letters are turned off.

| Setting | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `label` and `hint` | The visible name and the help text |
| `placeholder` | Light text shown while the box is empty. Never use it as the label |
| `rows` | How many lines tall the box starts. It starts at 4 lines |
| `resize` | `none` (the default), `vertical`, `horizontal`, `both` or `auto` |
| `required` | Adds a marker to the label. The marker is `*` unless a custom element changes it |
| `maxlength` and `minlength` | Limits. There is no counter, and `maxlength` stops typing without saying why |
| `readonly` and `disabled` | Show the text without letting people change it |
| `validation-message` and `auto-validate` | The text of a problem and when it appears |

With `resize="auto"` the box grows with the text up to 200 pixels, then scrolls.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The theme has no setting aimed at `ha-textarea`. These apply:

- The strong border colour is the line under the text at rest, `#0B0C0C` in light mode and `#FFFFFF` in dark mode.
- The theme’s primary colour is the line and label while the box has focus.
- The error line uses `#9A2B2B` in light mode and `#F0C6C6` in dark mode.
- Hint text uses the secondary text colour.

Do not expect a full dark box or a yellow focus ring around it. The look of the box, the spacing and the focus line are Home Assistant’s own.

**Names and problems.** The label and hint are drawn with the field. When the answer is invalid, the message appears in an area that is announced politely. The field itself is not marked as invalid, so name the field in the message: “Enter a description for this dashboard.” Keep what the person typed.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The native box gives paste, spellcheck, resize and the right keyboard without extra work.
- Home Assistant’s box can grow with the text, so long answers stay readable.
- A dashboard author cannot place either field in a card. They appear in dialogs and forms.
- There is no character counter. If a limit really exists, write it in the hint before the field.
- There is no automatic saving of drafts. Keep what people typed while another answer fails.
- Home Assistant does not mark the box as invalid for screen readers, so the message must name it.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Say who can read the text and where it appears: “Shown to everyone who can open this dashboard.” Do not store passwords, access tokens or private care instructions in a shared dashboard note. When another answer fails, keep the text people have typed and say clearly if anything is unsaved before they leave.

Do not impose a limit only to control card height. Use a [Character count](character-count.html) only for a real storage or delivery limit.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: every box has a visible label.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification, 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: the label says what to write, and an error says what to change.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: keep what people have typed.
- 3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Minimum): allow paste.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard: people can leave the box with Tab.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: the box must fit a 320 pixel screen and scroll or grow with larger text.

How to check a box: paste several lines, type line breaks and a very long answer, resize it, and fail another answer to see that the text stays.

